{"name":"Red Kite","hex":"#913228","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red kite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A predator bird of the kite (Accipitridae) family, Milvus milvus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_kite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Red kite","blocks":[{"text":"The red kite is a bird of prey in the family Accipitridae, which also includes many other diurnal raptors such as eagles, buzzards, and harriers. The species currently breeds only in Europe, though it formerly also bred in West Asia and Northwest Africa. Historically, it was only resident in the milder parts of its range in Western Europe and Northwestern Africa, whereas all or most red kites in Northern mainland Europe wintered to the south and west, some also reaching western Asia, but an increasing number of northern birds now remain in that region year-round. Vagrants have reached north to Finland and south to Israel, Libya and the Gambia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Milvus_milvus_R%28ThKraft%29.jpg/330px-Milvus_milvus_R%28ThKraft%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_kite"},{"title":"Red Kite (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Red Kite is the second solo studio album by British singer-songwriter Sarah Cracknell. It was released on 15 June 2015 via Cherry Red Records. Produced by Carwyn Ellis, Sebastian Lewsley and Mason Neely, it features guest appearances from Nicky Wire and The Rails. The album peaked at number 49 on the UK Albums Chart."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/Red_Kite_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Kite_(album)"},{"title":"Kite","blocks":[{"text":"A kite is a tethered heavier-than-air craft with wing surfaces that react against the air to create lift and drag forces. A kite consists of wings, tethers and anchors. Kites often have a bridle and tail to guide the face of the kite so the wind can lift it. Some kite designs do not need a bridle; box kites can have a single attachment point. A kite may have fixed or moving anchors that can balance the kite. The name is derived from the kite, the hovering bird of prey. There are several kite shapes, including the popular delta kite and diamond kite."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Kitesflying.jpg/330px-Kitesflying.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kite"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Milvus_milvus_distr02.png/500px-Milvus_milvus_distr02.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Milvus_milvus_distr02.png/500px-Milvus_milvus_distr02.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMilvus_milvus_distr02.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg/500px-Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg/1280px-Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg/500px-Red_Kite_in_Wales.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red Kite at Bwlch Nant yr Arian, Wales, a local feeding ground.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_Kite_in_Wales.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/White_Red_Kite_in_Flight_-_geograph.org.uk_-_687770.jpg/500px-White_Red_Kite_in_Flight_-_geograph.org.uk_-_687770.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/White_Red_Kite_in_Flight_-_geograph.org.uk_-_687770.jpg/500px-White_Red_Kite_in_Flight_-_geograph.org.uk_-_687770.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Leucistic form","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Red_Kite_in_Flight_-_geograph.org.uk_-_687770.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Red_Kite_%28Milvus_milvus%29_skull_at_the_Royal_Veterinary_College_anatomy_museum.JPG/500px-Red_Kite_%28Milvus_milvus%29_skull_at_the_Royal_Veterinary_College_anatomy_museum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Red_Kite_%28Milvus_milvus%29_skull_at_the_Royal_Veterinary_College_anatomy_museum.JPG/1280px-Red_Kite_%28Milvus_milvus%29_skull_at_the_Royal_Veterinary_College_anatomy_museum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Red_Kite_%28Milvus_milvus%29_skull_at_the_Royal_Veterinary_C